You wrote two pytest test functions for a CLI in an earlier pass. Both of
them fail immediately, on every platform, before ever invoking the CLI
under test, because of a bug in the test code itself — not the CLI. Fix
just this bug; the test logic and assertions are otherwise correct and
must not change.

## The two functions, as originally written

```python
@pytest.mark.skipif(sys.platform != "win32", reason="Windows encoding bug only")
@pytest.mark.parametrize("fmt", ["table", "md", "csv", "json"])
def test_windows_encoding_no_unicode_encode_error(tmp_path, fmt):
    file = tmp_path / "unicode_heading.md"
    file.write_text("## Flecha \u2192 \u00ab comillas \u00bb \u2014 guion\nBody text.\n")
    env = os.environ.copy()
    env.pop("PYTHONIOENCODING", None)
    result = subprocess.run(
        ["tokmd", str(file), "--platform", "claude-code", "--sections", "--format", fmt],
        capture_output=True,
        env=env,
    )
    assert result.returncode == 0
    stderr = result.stderr.decode("utf-8", errors="replace")
    assert "UnicodeEncodeError" not in stderr


@pytest.mark.skipif(sys.platform != "win32", reason="Windows encoding bug only")
def test_windows_encoding_arrow_survives(tmp_path):
    file = tmp_path / "unicode_heading.md"
    file.write_text("## Flecha \u2192 \u00ab comillas \u00bb \u2014 guion\nBody text.\n")
    env = os.environ.copy()
    env.pop("PYTHONIOENCODING", None)
    result = subprocess.run(
        ["tokmd", str(file), "--platform", "claude-code", "--sections", "--format", "table"],
        capture_output=True,
        env=env,
    )
    assert result.returncode == 0
    stdout = result.stdout.decode("utf-8")
    assert "\u2192" in stdout
```

## The symptom (crude, exactly as pytest printed it — the CLI is never reached)

```
tests\test_cli.py:273: in test_windows_encoding_arrow_survives
    file.write_text("## Flecha \u2192 \xab comillas \xbb \u2014 guion\nBody text.\n")
  C:\...\Lib\pathlib.py:1048: in write_text
    return f.write(data)
  C:\...\Lib\encodings\cp1252.py:19: in encode
    return codecs.charmap_encode(input,self.errors,encoding_table)[0]
UnicodeEncodeError: 'charmap' codec can't encode character '\u2192' in
position 10: character maps to <undefined>
```

`Path.write_text(...)` with no `encoding=` argument uses the platform's
default text encoding. On this project's Windows machine that default is
`cp1252`, which cannot represent `\u2192` (the arrow character the test
itself is trying to write into the fixture file) — so the test crashes
while setting up its own fixture, before the subprocess is ever started.
This has nothing to do with the CLI under test; it is a defect in the test
fixture's own file-writing call.

## The fix

Both `write_text` calls must pass `encoding="utf-8"` explicitly, so the
fixture file is written the same way regardless of the machine's default
locale encoding: `file.write_text(text, encoding="utf-8")`.

## Output format

Return exactly one fenced Python code block containing **only these two
corrected functions**, nothing else: no prose before or after, no other
functions, no new imports beyond what the two functions above already use
(`pytest`, `os`, `subprocess`, `sys` — already imported elsewhere in the
real file; do not repeat the imports in your answer). Keep every other
line of both functions byte-for-byte identical to the version above —
change only the two `write_text` calls.
